itten with  scythe on basically because it seems a  little complicated it's got to go  through a multi-step process can someone  come along and like pip install  something that was written using scythe  on yes and so it's a really interesting  question about deployment the good news  is that Python wheels have rescued us  from a lot of deployment problems so you  can deploy binaries much more easily and  it's a bit more complicated if you're  trying to pass along scythe on C code so  one of the really nice things about  scythe on is that if you have a C  compiler already installed you can write  your setup PI to accept scythe on C  files no get compiled on-the-fly pretty  easily with not much fuss to the user  it's a lot harder if you're deploying to  say for instance Windows where people  don't tend to have a compiler installed  and it's a big project for them to get  one if you're deploying to say a Linux  environment where you know there's  compilers readily accessible deploying  scythe on even if it's not precompiled  is pretty easy  was there another question out there  there was a hand up there somewhere hey  for the embedded side  how do external packages work so if you  if you were to embed a Python  interpreter how do you natively get the  you know num num PI and pandas and all  that other stuff is that an easy process  or is it a manual um it's it's easy once  you do things right so what at the  beginning when you initialize Python you  tell Python where it can find its  libraries and so it you need to provide  in addition to your embedded Python the  Python standard library because of  course a lot of python is implemented in  python itself and so just embedding a  Python interpreter into your executable  doesn't actually get you fully fledged  Python you have to provide the Python  runtime library and also the Python  standard library and in with that  standard library then you can supply all  the other good things that usually find  inside packages nump